## CMK Language & Compiler
<hr class="section-rule lvl-2">

A hands-on tutorial for the **CMK language** and its compiler: the concrete syntax, and how each construct *lowers* to Makefile.  CMK adds no new capability over plain `compose.mk`; it elevates existing idioms to native syntax.  That matters a *lot*, because notation is part of how we think.[^1]

{{ macros.img_link("compiler-seed-hosted.png", mkdocs, "95%", class=" ") }}

Under the hood the compiler is a two-layer program: a hand-written **seed** (the make/awk core, parsed first) and a growing **[`__hosted__`](#hosted-partition)** partition authored in CMK and lowered by compose.mk's own compiler.  The boundary is the seed's kind-system API, which every hosted kind is authored with; see the [Hosted Partition](#hosted-partition) and [Multistage](#multistage) appendices for how that second stage is bound.

