##### Compose-Context & Bind-Script
<hr class="section-rule lvl-5">
<a name=compose-context></a>
<a name=bind-script></a>

The last example inlines a compose file, and then dispatches targets.  How about using an external compose file, then running script-dispatch with a tool-container?  This example introduces two new idioms that can help.

Below, examples for `script1.sh` and `script2.sh` are equivalent, but use two different idioms:

{{site.embed_twin('demos/bind-script.mk', 'demos/cmk/bind-script.cmk', github=github, default='cmk', see_also=_+'/container-dispatch/#container-agnostic-dispatch', see_also_text='Container Dispatch: agnostic dispatch')}}

So why have two idioms if they are equivalent?  Well, both of these turn out to be pretty generic.  Since we'll encounter other variants later, it makes sense to introduce them side by side for some compare and contrast.

* **The decorator-style idiom** with `compose.bind.script` is most explicit, and one of several possible [bind declarations](#bind-declarations) that CMK supports.  This actually looks like a traditional Makefile target, but also adds a piece that looks like a python decorator.

* **The `in` machine-algebra** is a more generic form: a recipe body `(| .. |) in <service>` dispatches the block into a compose service.  The same `in` will come up again, because depending on what sits to its right, it can dispatch into a named `container` / `Dockerfile` instance or an anonymous `container(| img=.. entrypoint=.. |)`; so it works with any script, any interpreter, and containers that aren't managed by docker compose.

Although we've switched to an external compose file rather than embedded one, both forms work with [inlined services](#compose-file-example) too.

**Choosing between *bind-declarations* vs the `in` *machine-algebra*** is sometimes just a matter of taste, but there are some practical considerations too.  One advantage of using bind-declarations is that [they can be stacked](#bind-declarations).  Meanwhile, the `in`-form is easier to refactor later, for example if you're moving from compose-backed containers in external files to [inlined-Dockerfiles](#local-context-embedded-containers), etc.

<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

**Supported arguments** are the same for both the decorator style and the `in`-form.  Both forms optionally support forwarding environment variables, sending output to stderr instead of stdout, etc.  See here [^5] for additional explanation re: `env` and `quiet`.

{% include "includes/args/compose.bind.script.md" %}

See [this demo]({{_}}/demos/ai/#dueling-philosophers) for a concrete example using multiple arguments.

##### Compose-Context & Bind-Target
<hr class="section-rule lvl-5">
<a name=bind-target></a>

Quick-and-dirty scripting is often convenient, but working with targets makes it much easier to leverage composition.  For this you can use `compose.bind.target`.  

This idiom binds together a given container, a "public" target and a "private" target.  The public-target is the main interface; the "private" target has the same name but uses a "self." prefix and always runs inside the named container.

{{site.embed_twin('demos/bind-target.mk', 'demos/cmk/bind-target.cmk', github=github, default='cmk', see_also=_+'/container-dispatch/#container-agnostic-dispatch', see_also_text='Container Dispatch: agnostic dispatch')}}

Again we import services here instead of inlining them, but `compose.bind.target` works in either case.

<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

**Supported arguments** for `compose.bind.target` include the ability to optionally forward environment variables, sending output to stderr instead of stdout, etc.  See here [^5] for additional explanation re: `env` and `quiet`.

{% include "includes/args/compose.bind.target.md" %}

The `(| .. |)` blocks and the `in` machine-algebra above are powered by *sugar*, a block-structured cousin of dialects that lowers source into Makefile define-blocks while giving them extra semantics.  The mechanics (and how to redefine them) live on the [Dialects &amp; Sugar]({{_}}/cmk/metaprogramming/#about-sugar) page.

#### Polyglot Examples
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

CMK particularly shines at cleaning up the idioms you can see in the [polyglot index]({{_}}), and almost every example is more clear and concise.  

##### Foreign Code Objects
<hr class="section-rule lvl-5">

The example below introduces the difference between bound vs unbound code, i.e. whether or not an interpreter has been declared.

Bound code (an interpreter *is* declared) is written with the first-class [`polyglot`]({{_}}/demos/polyglots) keyword, which carries the image and entrypoint arguments alongside its body, or it is bound to a specific `container` / `machine` instance via that instance's `.polyglot`.  Unbound code (no interpreter declared: a "trivial" code-object with only `.preview`/`.to.file`) is created with the `code.unbound` keyword, independent of any machine.

An anonymous inline `container` (used with `in`) takes its keyword arguments in the banana body; a named `container` or `Dockerfile` instance carries them in a leading paren instead, where a quoted value may span multiple words, and the `in` dispatch then just names it.

The `in` clause need not sit on the same line as the closing `|)`.  It may stay inline, or use a trailing `\` for a Makefile-style continuation that carries the dispatch onto the following line(s):

{# ```Makefile
# inline
(| .. |) in container(| img=alpine entrypoint=bash |)

# backslash-continued
(| .. |) \
    in container(| img=alpine entrypoint=bash |)
``` #}

{{site.embed_twin('demos/code-objects.mk', 'demos/cmk/code-objects.cmk', github=github, default='cmk')}}

Since the `in` machine-algebra is generic enough to already handle the polyglot use-case by naming a container carrying its own image and entrypoint, why add another syntax?

There are some differences, including the scaffolded `preview` target.  But the main difference is that this approach to polyglots is *intentionally* pretty minimal.

* Creating a code-object off a plain target tends to result in designs that *could* use a container, but doesn't strictly require one, and usually makes things more friendly for composition.  That allows you to use host tools if available and fall-back to containers, or make dynamic decisions about the image to use.

* Dispatching a body straight into a container only supports container/interpreter arguments.  That makes environment-variable passthrough more awkward, and therefore encourages only pipe-based I/O if any is required.

