{{ macros.section(4, "Bind Declarations", id="bind-declarations") }}
<a name=bind-declarations></a>

Bind-declarations were introduced in [the compose file example](#compose-file-example), but they show up in a few places.  You can think of them as a type of "target decorator", similar to function-decorators in python.  Bind declarations in CMK-Lang use `@` at column 0, exactly like a python decorator.  Because make's `@` is a recipe-only prefix (tab-indented), a `@` at the left margin never collides; so the compiler tells them apart by position.  As with a python decorator, the `@` line (or lines, when [stacked](#stacking-bind-declarations)) goes immediately *above* the target it applies to, with no blank line in between.

One use-case of bind-declarations is to bind variables to values, basically providing support for keyword-arguments, [as seen elsewhere]({{_}}/standard-lib/#argument-parsing).  

The equivalent idiom is much more readable in cmk-lang:

{{site.embed_twin('demos/kwarg-parsing.mk', 'demos/cmk/kwarg-parsing.cmk', github=github, default='cmk', see_also=_+'/standard-lib/#argument-parsing', see_also_text='Argument Parsing')}}

