{{ macros.section(4, "Capture Operator", id="capture-operator") }}
<a name=capture-operator></a>

The `<-` operator *captures* a command's output.  In a **recipe** (tab-indented) it binds a shell variable: the ubiquitous backtick-assignment idiom without the backtick noise; at **module** scope (column 0) it captures at parse time into a make variable via a shell-expansion assignment.  The arrow is *adjacent* (a spaced `< -` stays a shell redirect) and spacing around it is free (`x<-y` == `x <- y`).  The useful part is that the right-hand side runs through the dialect first.  So for example:

```Makefile {.snippet .language-makefile}

# CMK Capture:
x <- this.t  

# Lowered to Makefile:
x=`${make} t`
```

The whole RHS is captured up to the next shell separator (one of `;`, `&&`, `||`), a trailing line-continuation, or end-of-line.[^defskip]

Because the RHS is lowered first, it can be any [call-form](#call-forms) or `cmk.<macro>(...)` macro-callform, not just a bare command.  For example, the RHS can be a receiver query, or `cmk.__locals__()` to capture this recipe's locals as JSON.  That second case is the natural pairing: since `<-` produces ordinary (non-exported) shell variables, those captures are exactly what `__locals__()` reflects (see the **Target Locals** entry in the [Quick Reference]({{_}}/cmk/quickref)).

{{ macros.section(4, "Triple-Quoted Forms", id="triple-quote-literals") }}
<a name=triple-quote-literals></a>

CMK has 3 triple-quoted literal forms.  Each emits its own body as text (handy for feeding a snippet or whole program into a pipeline without the usual quoting/escaping hassle), and each supports multi-line content.[^defskip]

They differ only in *what gets interpolated*.  One caveat applies to all three: the body can't contain an internal run of three-or-more of its own delimiter character.

##### Triple-Quote (Singles)
<hr class="section-rule lvl-5">

The form for `'''…'''` is literal (to the shell).  Internal quotes and `%` are taken literally.  However the usual two-layer expansion applies:

* **Make** variables and functions DO expand (variable reads and macro calls alike)
* **Shell** constructs do NOT expand: a shell variable, a command-substitution, and an arithmetic/subshell form are all passed through verbatim (the single quotes protect them).

```Makefile {.snippet .language-makefile}
X := MAKE_VALUE
demo:
  @echo start
  '''mk=${X} sh=$${USER}'''
  @# printed: mk=MAKE_VALUE sh=${USER}  (the shell var stays literal)
```

**Lowering:** The compiler lowers this form as a **single-quoted** `printf '%s' '…'`.

!!! note "Reserved positions: `'''…'''` as a docstring"
    Three positions give `'''…'''` a *different*, now-standard meaning, so it is **not** printed there:

    * as the **first line of a recipe** it is a **target docstring**, lowered to a doc-comment and surfaced through the `__target__.__doc__` reader and in [generated help]({{_}}/cli-help/#docstring-style);
    * at **column&nbsp;0** (bare, module level) it is a **module docstring**, lowered to a `__doc__` block and surfaced through the module's `__name__.__doc__` reader;
    * as the **first line of a banana body** (a class, `dsl`, module, or namespace declaration) it is that instance's docstring, lifted out to a sibling `<name>.__doc__` and qualified for a nested member.  This is uniform across cooked and raw bodies, so a raw `jqlang q(| '''…''' … |)` and a cooked `cmk.class Foo[| '''…''' … |]` both surface a `q.__doc__` / `Foo.__doc__` reader.

    The literal-string form above is therefore any `'''…'''` that is **not** in one of those three positions (e.g. a later recipe line, or an argument to a call-form).  That is why the example puts a recipe line ahead of it.

##### Triple-Quote (Backticks & Doubles)
<hr class="section-rule lvl-5">

The forms for `"""…"""` and ` ```…``` ` are both interpolating.  These two are **equivalent**; just use whichever form doesn't clash with the body.  On top of the make expansion above, the **shell** layer now interpolates as well: a shell variable, a command-substitution, and an arithmetic/subshell form all expand at runtime.  (Single quote = literal, double quote = interpolating, just like in shell.)

```Makefile {.snippet .language-makefile}
X := MAKE_VALUE
demo:
  """mk=${X} sh=$${USER}"""
  @# output is mk=MAKE_VALUE sh=<value of $USER>
```

**Lowering:**  Both of these triple-quote forms lower to a **double-quoted** `printf '%s' "…"`.  

