#### Receiver Form
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">
<a name=receiver-form></a>
<a name=receivers></a>

The primary [call-form](#call-form) anchors are explicit.  A **receiver** is a more implicit third option: a name brought into scope by a [declaration]({{_}}/macros/#declare-namespace).

This form covers various first-class constructs like a [channel]({{_}}/macros/#declare-namespace), a [module]({{_}}/cmk/modules), a [polyglot]({{_}}/demos/polyglots), or a [container]({{_}}/demos/matrioshka).   After declaration, the name is implied, but otherwise the standard call-form grammar applies.

##### Lowering & Quick Example
<hr class="section-rule lvl-5">

```Makefile {.snippet .language-makefile}
# declares a channel named `inbox`, registering it as a receiver
channel inbox(| [] |)

# -> printf '%s' "type=login user=alice" | $(if $(filter file override,$(origin inbox.emit)),$(call inbox.emit),${make} inbox.emit)
inbox.emit["""type=login user=alice"""]

# logins=`${make} inbox.filter.field_equal/type,login`
logins <- inbox.filter.field_equal/type,login
```

Since the declaration builds targets dynamically, the 2nd form above is just a small change over the target-calling form we've already met: the same send written with an explicit target anchor on `inbox.emit`.

!!! tip "OOP?"
    This resembles message-passing OOP since the *receiver* is the "object" a message is sent to and `receiver.method[...]` deliberately reads that way.  But it's simpler than that.  There are no instances, no inheritance, and no `self`.  
    
    A receiver is a compile-time **namespace**, not a runtime object, and the send is resolved by static name-rewriting rather than dynamic dispatch.  Any state involved lives *outside* the "object" (in this case a channel's queue is a stack-file, not a field of `inbox`).  Think *namespaced message-send syntax* rather than classes.. this gives the first-class look-and-feel without a real object model.

A receiver-send fires **only** when all three hold, which is what keeps it from meddling with ordinary names:

* The name was **declared** (an undeclared `foo.bar[...]` is left untouched),
* It sits at a **word boundary** (the start of a recipe word, not mid-token), and
* It is followed by a **call-suffix**: `(`*args*`)`, `[`*stream*`]`, `/`*arg*, or an adjacent triple-quote.

So a target *definition* (`inbox.seed:`), a parametric def (`inbox.scan/%:`), a bare prerequisite
(`__main__: inbox.seed`), and anything inside a `define`-block are all left as-is. A legacy
send written with an explicit target anchor on `inbox.emit` still works and is never double-anchored.

Receiver names are discovered from the file's own declarations at compile time, by naming convention.  Every form that **introduces a bindable name** is enumerated in one place (the compiler's receiver pre-scan), which doubles as the de-facto grammar of cmk-lang *declaration forms*:

| form | introduces | example |
|---|---|---|
| `[ctor] NAME(\| … \|)`, `NAME[\| … \|]`, `NAME{\| … \|}` | a [banana block](#banana-forms) name (raw / deep-cooked / pragma family); a leading constructor word (`channel`, `agent`, `container`, …) creates that kind | `channel inbox(\| … \|)` |
| `namespace=NAME` | a renamed [module]({{_}}/cmk/modules) import (the member prefix) | `import.module(def=lib namespace=fs)` |
| `… def=NAME` | a [polyglot]({{_}}/demos/polyglots) or single-image [container]({{_}}/demos/matrioshka) block name | `code(def=parse)` |
| `import … as NAME[, …]` | an aliased [import]({{_}}/cmk/modules) | `import x/y as fs` |
| `open NAME[, …]`, `import NAME[, …]` | an open / import module list | `open a, b` |

Because this pre-scan is the single place the whole set lives (the parser stages each handle their own form, and the highlighter grammars mirror it), adding a declaration form means updating it too.  Multi-service compose files are the exception: their service names are only known at runtime, so they keep the explicit `<svc> ᐉ <target>` dispatch.

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/channel.cmk', github=github)}}

{{ macros.section(3, "Blocks, Layout &amp; Config", id="blocks-layout-config") }}

{{ macros.section(4, "Block References", id="block-references") }}
<a name=block-references></a>
<a name=block-refs></a><a name=block-ref></a>
<a name=blockrefs></a> <a name=blockref></a>

A `define`-block keeps a snippet (a script, a program, a query) in one named place; a
**block-ref glyph** then feeds that block straight into a command, with no temp-file dance.

{% include "includes/blockref-algebra.md" %}

{{site.embed_twin('demos/blockrefs.mk', 'demos/cmk/blockrefs.cmk', github=github, default='cmk')}}

!!! info "Tempfile Lifecycles"
    The two glyphs have very different lifecycles.  The `⬦` form lowers to a process-substitution (`<(…)`): the shell owns the transient FD and tears it down automatically when the command exits, so there's nothing on disk to manage.  The `⬥` form lowers to a command-substitution that runs `mktemp` *every time the recipe line executes*, writes the block to that file, and substitutes the path into your command.  A new file is created per evaluation (so re-running a target, or referencing the same block twice, yields distinct paths).  These files land in a run-scoped scratch location (a `.tmp.cmk.brf.*` name keyed on the run id) and are **auto-removed at the end of the run** by the supervisor, so you don't have to manage them.  The manual `mk.clean` target is available as a backstop if a run is hard-killed before teardown.

{{ macros.section(4, "Banana Forms", id="banana-forms") }}

A **banana block** `NAME(| body |)` is a native, generic *declare*: the body lowers to `define NAME .. endef`, then a constructor (a trailing `as C`, or a leading dotpath) is applied.  It is the block-structured sibling of the [triple-quoted forms](#triple-quote-literals) and [block-references](#block-references), and it composes with the [call-form channels](#call-forms) you just met (`[stream]`, `(args)`, `{env}`).

{{ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/banana-blocks.cmk', github=github) }}

The block is only the atom of a larger family: multi-body currying, anonymous lambda-lift, cooked (compile-twice) blocks, and user-defined `declare.*` constructors.  The full reference, with a demo per idiom, is on the [Banana Forms]({{_}}/cmk/banana) page.

{{ macros.section(4, "Recipe-Body Joining", id="recipe-body-joining") }}
<a name=recipe-body-joining></a>

The newline-separated lines of a recipe body are joined into a single shell with ` && ` connectors *by default*.  A body written one-statement-per-line therefore shares state (variables, `cd`) across its lines and stops on the first failure. The joining is conservative and should not surprise recipes that already manage their own control-flow: a line that already ends in a connector (`&&`, `||`, `|`) just continues to the next, a line you split yourself with a trailing `\` stays one command, and `-`- or `+`-prefixed lines stand alone.

The ` && ` default is a *policy*, not a law: it is overridable per-file with the [`recipe_join` compiler pragma]({{_}}/cmk/pragmas/#pragma-recipe_join).

{{ macros.section(4, "Compiler Pragmas", id="compiler-pragmas") }}
<a name=compiler-pragmas></a>

[Dialects]({{_}}/cmk/metaprogramming/#about-dialects) and [sugar]({{_}}/cmk/metaprogramming/#about-sugar) let a file extend the *syntax* it's written in.  A **pragma** is the third member of that family: a leading-comment `cmk_pragma :::` JSON hint that sets *per-program settings* in-source, in lieu of piling environment variables onto the shebang: the [`recipe_join`]({{_}}/cmk/pragmas/#pragma-recipe_join) connector, extra [compiler stages]({{_}}/cmk/pragmas/#pragma-compiler_pre_post), [REPL mode]({{_}}/cmk/pragmas/#pragma-repl), the [boot lifecycle]({{_}}/runtime/#lifecycle-boot-settings), and more.

The full catalog lives on its own page, **[Compiler Pragmas]({{_}}/cmk/pragmas)**: the `cmk_pragma` marker and resolution rules, the [`__pragma__` protocol]({{_}}/cmk/pragmas/#pragma-protocol), a summary table, and a card per pragma.

#### Indentation
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">
<a name=indentation></a>

Plain Makefiles require recipe lines to begin with a literal **tab**.  CMK relaxes this to **python-style indentation**: a target body may be indented with **tabs or spaces**, and the compiler normalizes a consistently space-indented body to the tab Make needs.  The rules, like Python's, are about *consistency*:

{{ site.contract_card([
  ['tabs', 'Passed through unchanged (you keep Make&rsquo;s &ldquo;extra tab = literal command content&rdquo; behaviour).'],
  ['spaces', 'Must use the <i>same</i> indent on every line; rewritten to a single leading tab.'],
  ['mixed / mismatched', 'Mixing tabs and spaces <i>within one indent</i> (&ldquo;mixed&rdquo;), or inconsistent amounts of space-indentation in one body (&ldquo;mismatched&rdquo;), is an <b>error</b>: the compiler stops with a clear message, much like Python&rsquo;s <code>TabError</code> / <code>IndentationError</code>.'],
], title='Consistency rules') }}

Indentation is normalized as the **last** preprocessing step, after [sugar
blocks]({{_}}/cmk/metaprogramming/#about-sugar) have been lowered.  See the {{macros.repo_link('demos/cmk/space-indented.cmk', name='space-indented demo', github=github)}}.

#### The `cmk` prelude (declaration keywords)

The declaration keywords (`class`, `constructor`, `container`, `machine`, `dsl`, `namespace`) are **not** bare top-level names.  They live in a small built-in module, `cmk`, qualified as `cmk.class`, `cmk.container`, and so on.  The qualified forms are **always available**:

```make
cmk.class Box[| ${self}.x = 1 |]
```

The bare spelling is *opt-in*: a program pulls the keywords in with an ordinary import statement (`from cmk import *`), exactly like Python.  Until it does, `class`/`container`/… stay free for the program's own grammar, and reaching a bare keyword without pulling it in is an error.  The star form, its `except`/selective variants, and the older `open cmk` alias are the same forms every core module supports; see the [import statements]({{_}}/cmk/modules/#the-declaration-family) reference for the full grammar.

The one compiler-specific mechanic: a qualified `cmk` keyword survives compilation as a **literal ctor** because the macro anchor (the calling convention behind a macro-callform like `cmk.log(..)`) is a structural, *banana-aware* stage (`cmkanchor`, run right after `dialect`): a qualified `cmk` name in the ctor position of a banana head is left intact for the sugar stage, while every other macro-anchored name still lowers to a macro call.  The list/callform spelling still means "call `class`" and needs a prior `from cmk import *`; the always-on qualified form is the banana spelling shown above.

