#### Related Reading
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

Other related documentation includes: The [more basic interpreter usage]({{_}}/signals/#inheritance-interpreters), which allows for inheritance of the entire `compose.mk` standard library and signals, but skips compilation and does **not** allow syntactic extensions.  The [packaging support]({{_}}/demos/packaging) generates executables, but *isn't* compilation.  And there's also [a few thoughts about style]({{_}}/style).

!!! terminology "In compiler terms"
    CMK is a **reflective, self-extending, partially self-hosting transpiler with a near-meta-circular runtime VM**:

    - **reflective**: a program reads its own compile-time manifest and runtime machine as first-class self-models ([`__pragma__` / `__plugins__` / `__modules__`]({{_}}/plugins/overview#program-self-models), [`__vm__`]({{_}}/modules/virtual-machine#self-model-steering)).
    - **self-extending**: a program (or an imported plugin) adds compiler stages from inside its own source, and the compiler invokes itself to discover them ([`compiler_pre`/`compiler_post` stage chain]({{_}}/cmk/pragmas/#pragma-compiler_pre_post), [two-pass pragma stacking]({{_}}/cmk/pragmas/#pragma-plugin_pragma_allowed)).
    - **self-hosting (partial)**: a growing region of compose.mk's own standard library (the [`__hosted__` partition](#hosted-partition)) is authored in cmk-lang and lowered by compose.mk's *own* compiler at include-time, so the program now compiles a slice of itself.
    - **near-meta-circular runtime**: the [`__vm__` CEK machine]({{_}}/modules/virtual-machine) interprets a goal-stream using the host `make`'s own dispatch, rather than building a separate evaluator.

    Caveat: this is a *partial* bootstrap, and still a transpiler rather than a meta-circular *evaluator*. The core compiler (and the Makefile-subset seed it rides in) never compiles itself; but the [`__hosted__` partition](#hosted-partition) (a growing slice of the stdlib) **is** authored in CMK and compiled by compose.mk itself.

The live default dialect and sugar are dumped from source on the [Dialects &amp; Sugar appendix]({{_}}/cmk/metaprogramming/#appendix).

#### Compile or Transpile?
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">
<a name=compilation-vs-transpilation></a>

For all the nerds out there, the honest answer has three parts:

1. **Not just a transpiler.**  The CMK -> Makefile *backend* is source-to-source, so **that step** looks like transpiling.  But a transpiler is a syntactic skin whose semantics are just the target's, whereas the CMK front-end *lowers*: an ordered, [extensible stage-pipeline]({{_}}/cmk/pragmas/#pragma-compiler_pre_post) realizing semantics `make` has no concept of ([receiver dispatch](#receiver-form), [block-ref lifecycles](#block-references), [recipe-join policy](#recipe-body-joining), [python-style indentation](#indentation), the [`<-` operator](#capture-operator), pragma-driven config).  And since [dialects &amp; sugar]({{_}}/cmk/metaprogramming) can redefine that syntax in-source, `make` no longer *defines* CMK; it's the compile target and runtime substrate.  So the honest label is **a compiler whose backend emits Makefile** (much as ClojureScript's backend emits JavaScript), not a Makefile pretty-printer.
2. **Interpretation is compilation *plus* execution.**  `cmk run` is that same compilation **plus supervised execution**, a runtime that owns an eval loop and a control model `make` doesn't provide: the [trampoline dispatch loop]({{_}}/runtime/#flat-dispatch), first-class [continuations]({{_}}/runtime/#continuations), and the optional [CEK virtual machine]({{_}}/runtime/#virtual-machine).  See the [Runtime]({{_}}/runtime) page for that layer.
3. **Compilation is not packaging.**  If you were expecting "compilation" to create an executable artifact, that's more like [packaging]({{_}}/demos/packaging): see [self-extracting executables]({{_}}/demos/packaging/#makeself-reference) and [guests &amp; forks]({{_}}/standard-lib/#forks-guests-and-payloads).

#### Templating Engine
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">
<a name=templating-engine></a>

Underneath the seed's kind-system sits a small **string-templating macro engine**, `m5`: essentially an [m4][^m4] macro processor rebuilt in `make`.  Every higher layer (the seed itself, banana-block lowering, `self`-attribute desugaring, the [hosted partition](#hosted-partition)) is written in its vocabulary, so it's the substrate the whole compiler rides on.

Its guiding idea is that **notation simulates a type system**.  A handful of sigils name what an operation *does*, so a glance at a name tells you its pole, and whole classes of `make` mistakes (reading an undefined variable, or evaluating text that was meant as data) become visible right at the call site.

{{ site.contract_card([
  ['?', '<b>read</b>: a variable&rsquo;s raw value (<code>m5?</code>, <code>m5.defined?</code>).'],
  ['!', '<b>force / eval</b>: evaluate text as Makefile (<code>m5!</code>, <code>m5.def!</code>).'],
  ['%', '<b>fill</b>: substitute one hole in a template.'],
  ['/', '<b>fold</b>: map a fill over a context.'],
  ['[..]', '<b>index</b>: positional / keyword argument access (<code>m5[1]</code>, <code>m5[def]</code>).'],
], title='Sigil vocabulary') }}

Two interpolation domains are kept deliberately separate (m4's `changequote`): the Make layer, and an awk-side `@@…@@` layer used while the engine generates the compiler's awk.  The everyday face of all this is the `m5|` read-or-default primitive (an `ifdef`-with-fallback read); the `m5` block in `compose.mk` is the authoritative catalog of the rest.

#### IR Details
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">
<a name=ir-details></a>

Between passes the compiler threads a few **internal sentinels** as a lightweight IR: invisible glyphs a user never types, emitted by one stage and resolved by a later one:

* macro-callforms normalize to a sentinel-prefixed form `؆X(...)` so later regex passes don't trip on a literal macro anchor; the late `call` stage turns that sentinel form into an ordinary macro call, and the trailing `unsentinel` stage restores any surviving `؆` back to the macro anchor.
* `@name` decorators normalize to a plain macro callform (there is no reserved namespace; a decorator is just a macro applied above a target), which then rides the same `؆` callform pipeline as the bullet above down to an ordinary macro call.
* The [block-ref](#block-references) glyphs `⬦`/`⬥` double as sentinels: a `(| .. |)[stream]` trailer *emits* `⬥` for the block-ref pass to materialize.

And one invariant every pass obeys: it is **inert inside `define`-blocks and comments**.  So embedded foreign source (a python `'''docstring'''` or `@decorator`, a shell `x <- y`) is copied through verbatim, never rewritten.

#### Multistage
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">
<a name=multistage></a>

CMK is a **staged**[^msp] language: source lowers to Makefile, and that lowered program can lower *more* CMK at run time.  A `(| .. |)` banana (or any `define`) is source-as-data, a **quasiquote**[^qq], and running a second lowering pass over it (lower a `define`, then run it) is the **cooking** idiom; the [Banana Forms]({{_}}/cmk/banana/#banana-cooked) page is the worked example.

That second stage never happens implicitly: the main compile (`cmk run` / `cmk compile`) is exactly ONE pass, inert inside `define`-blocks (see [IR Details](#ir-details)), so a banana stays *data* until you opt in through one of these interfaces:

!!! tip "<strong>Opt-in interfaces</strong>"
	* **`lang.transpile`** -- the primitive: CMK on stdin, an includable Makefile fragment out.  Everything below builds on it.
	* **`native_target`** / **`cmk.cook`** -- JIT-compile a `define` on first build, then content-cache it; callable from a vanilla `include compose.mk`.
	* **`import.module`** -- import a `NAME[\| .. \|]` banana as a namespaced module, lowered by default.
	* **[`define __hosted__`](#hosted-partition)** -- compose.mk's own CMK partition, lowered and cached: the self-hosting[^sh] slice (its own [appendix](#hosted-partition)).

`lang.transpile` is the one to reach for directly, and the clearest way to watch a construct *lower*: pipe CMK in, read the Makefile fragment back.  A `<-` capture becomes a shell assignment, and a macro-callform becomes an ordinary macro call:

```bash {.cli_example}
# lang.transpile: CMK on stdin, an includable Makefile fragment out
$ cat <<'CMK' | ./compose.mk lang.transpile
build:
	user <- whoami
	cmk.log.io(built by $${user})
CMK
```
```bash {.cli_output}
build:
	user=`whoami` && \
	$(call log.io,built by $${user})
```

So multistage is **opt-in, per-site, and cached**: you decide exactly where a second stage runs, and (for `native_*`) it compiles once and reuses forever.

#### Hosted Partition
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">
<a name=hosted-partition></a>

The **`__hosted__` partition** is where compose.mk compiles a slice of *itself*.  The [seed](#implementation-details) (the make/awk core, parsed first) is hand-written Makefile, but a growing region of the standard library is authored in CMK-Lang inside a single `define __hosted__ ... endef` and lowered by compose.mk's *own* compiler.  It is the concrete form of the "partially self-hosting" claim above: the same [`lang.transpile`](#multistage) pass that lowers your code lowers the stdlib's hosted kinds too.

Compilation happens at include-time, wired through `make`'s makefile-remaking:

!!! terminology "<strong>Cold, warm, cached</strong>"
	1. A **cold** parse lowers `__hosted__` once, writes the result to a content-addressed cache (keyed on the partition's source hash), and restarts `make` a single time to pick it up.
	1. A **warm** parse is just a hash check plus an `-include` of that cache, so nothing recompiles.
	1. The cache is regenerable and remade **only** when the source hash changes: editing hosted code triggers exactly one cold rebuild, while unrelated runs stay warm.

Because the boundary is the seed's kind-system API (`lang.ctor!` + `cmk.class`), a hosted kind is authored exactly like any user kind; the partition is not privileged syntax, only the largest in-tree consumer of the [multistage](#multistage) interfaces.

